    <div style="text-align: right;">
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; margin: 0;">IMPROVE</p>
    </div>
  </header>

  <div style="display: flex; gap: 40px;">
    <div style="flex: 2;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 15px;">Wildfire and Prescribed Fires Acres Burned - 1990 to 2011</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">Figure 1: Acres burned by wild and prescribed fires from 1990 to 2011. This chart illustrates the trend in fire activity over the past two decades, showing fluctuations influenced by climate variability and land management practices.</p>
      <img src="placeholder_figure1.png" alt="Figure 1" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px;">

      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Fire Emissions and Air Quality</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">Fire emissions, in the form of smoke, volatile organic hydrocarbons (VOCs), and particulate matter (PM), are significant contributors to regional air quality degradation. The combustion process releases a complex mixture of gases and aerosols that can travel long distances, impacting air quality far from the source.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">The contribution of wildfires to ozone and particulate matter is a major area of research. Understanding the chemical pathways and transport mechanisms is crucial for developing effective air quality management strategies.</p>
      <img src="placeholder_figure2.png" alt="Figure 2" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px;">
    </div>
    <div style="flex: 1;">
      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-bottom: 15px;">Fire Contribution to NOx and PM</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">Figure 4: The panel above shows chemical transport model estimate of ambient ozone concentrations while the panel below shows the contribution of fires to fine particulate matter.</p>
      <img src="placeholder_figure4.png" alt="Figure 4" style="max-width: 100%; height: auto; margin-top: 10px;">

      <h3 style="font-size: 16pt; margin-top: 30px; margin-bottom: 15px;">Modeling and Assessment</h3>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">Modeling and assessment tools are needed to rapidly estimate the relative contribution of different sources to ambient air pollution. These tools help scientists quantify the impact of events like wildfires on regional air quality.</p>
      <p style="font-size: 10pt; line-height: 1.6;">The research presented here utilizes advanced atmospheric chemistry models to simulate the dispersion and chemical transformation of fire emissions. This allows for a more accurate assessment of the health risks associated with wildfire smoke.</p>
    </div>
  </div>
</div>

